$DIR/main.p:15:19: the tasks started at main.p:15 may write the same element of out
    Each task writes out[i] at main.p:15, and several of them run at the same
    time. Tasks may write one array or slice at once only when each writes
    elements no other can reach, and the compiler can see that only for x[i], or
    a window such as x[i*k:(i+1)*k] or x[i:i+k], where i is the variable of the
    loop that starts them (each task takes its own i), or for x[:m] and x[m:].
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Index by the loop variable:
        s.Task(func() { out[i] = f(i) })
    Or give each task a window and index within it:
        row := out[i*w : (i+1)*w]
    Or send the results on a channel and store them in the function that starts
    the tasks.
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3403" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:9: results[0] is read here while the task started at main.p:24 may write it
    The task started at main.p:24 writes results[i] at main.p:24, and s waits
    for it where its block ends, at main.p:27. Meanwhile results[0] is read at
    main.p:26 in first, so the two may run at the same time, one of them
    writing: a data race.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Wait for the tasks before using results[0]:
        s.Wait()
    Or lock one sync.Mutex around every use of it, in the tasks and here:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:34:19: total is written by the tasks started at main.p:34 at the same time as each other
    Each call of s.Task at main.p:34 starts a task, and they run at the same
    time. Each writes total (main.p:34), and another reads it (main.p:34). Two
    tasks using one place at once, one of them writing, is a data race: an
    update can be lost, and a value larger than a word can be read half written.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Lock a mutex around every use of total:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type such as atomic.Int64.
    Or give each task its own element: results[i] = ..., where i is the loop
    variable.
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:51:10: cannot move b here: the task started at main.p:50 may still be using it
    The task started at main.p:50 writes b.n at main.p:50, and s waits for it
    where its block ends, at main.p:52, so the task could use b after it has
    moved or been freed.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    Wait for the task first:
        s.Wait()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3404" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:59:19: the task started at main.p:59 changes n, but has its own copy of it, so the change is not seen at main.p:60
    n is declared in a block that may end before s waits for the task
    (main.p:58), so the task takes its own copy of n when s.Task runs, as each
    iteration of a Go loop has its own variables. The task changes its copy at
    main.p:59, and taken uses n again at main.p:60, where it still holds its old
    value.
    Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector,
    however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its
    function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it
    started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and
    the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first.
    If only the task needs n, declare it inside the function literal; if taken
    needs the result, give each task its own element of a variable declared
    before the loop:
        results[i] = ...
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3407" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:80:36: Map calls f from several tasks at once, and the function literal passed here writes calls
    Map starts tasks that call f, several at the same time. The function literal
    passed here writes calls at main.p:80 with no lock held, so the calls would
    write it at the same time: a data race.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium
    puroutines run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free
    of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Use an atomic type for it, or lock a mutex around the write:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3406" for a longer explanation)
